WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.870 --> 00:00:06.170 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Hi, I&#x27;m Shi Feng, a PhD student at Cornell
University.

2
00:00:07.050 --> 00:00:10.450 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
In this video, we will talk about the optimal
matching problem.

3
00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:17.670 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
In particular, I want to share the main result
and ideas from these

4
00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:22.630 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
two papers, both studying how the optimal
matching cost fluctuates

5
00:00:22.630 --> 00:00:24.390 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
when the points are random.

6
00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:28.370 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
They address different dimensions using very
different methods.

7
00:00:29.090 --> 00:00:34.430 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
The first, written with Gilles Mordant, treats
dimensions two, three and 4.

8
00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:37.710 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
The second treats dimensions 5 and higher.

9
00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:42.920 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Here is the setup of the optimal matching
problem.

10
00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:45.880 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Imagine a company with several factories.

11
00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:49.720 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Their locations are chosen randomly in this
square.

12
00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:54.540 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Workers live throughout the square, spread
evenly across it.

13
00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:57.240 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
We treat them as a continuous population.

14
00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:03.950 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
The question is simple. How should we assign
workers to factories?

15
00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:07.920 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
To see what an assignment means, look at this
worker&#x27;s commute.

16
00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:11.820 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
An assignment gives a destination to every
worker.

17
00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:16.100 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
This divides the square into regions, one for
each factory.

18
00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:17.860 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
We call this a partition.

19
00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:23.100 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
The colored regions collect the workers who go
to the same factory.

20
00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:26.580 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Everyone in the green regions goes to the green
factory.

21
00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:30.340 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
But not every assignment is allowed.

22
00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:32.660 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
There is a staffing requirement.

23
00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:38.280 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Every factory needs the same share of workers.
With six factories,

24
00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:39.760 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
each get one

25
00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:45.200 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
sixth of the population because the workers are
spread evenly their regions

26
00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:50.400 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
must have equal area for example if every worker
goes to

27
00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:52.600 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
the nearest factory some

28
00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:58.060 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
factories may receive more workers and others
fewer this would not meet our

29
00:01:58.060 --> 00:01:59.360 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
equal staffing requirement.

30
00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:06.160 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Now let&#x27;s define the transportation cost we
want to optimize.

31
00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:11.840 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
If a worker lives at x and travel to the factory
at xi,

32
00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:15.720 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
one natural cost is the distance dx xi.

33
00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:19.180 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
This is a well-established model but the
corresponding

34
00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:21.600 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
fluctuation problem is still open.

35
00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:27.560 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
In this project, we instead use the squared
distance dx xi squared.

36
00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:29.940 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
This is also a reasonable model.

37
00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:35.680 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
A ten-hour flight can feel more than twice as
exhausting as a five-hour flight.

38
00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:40.720 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
With this cost in place, we can now choose the
best assignment.

39
00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:44.640 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
After fixing the randomly chosen factory
locations,

40
00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:49.200 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
we can partition the square into regions, one
for each factory.

41
00:02:49.580 --> 00:02:54.920 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
We want the smallest total transportation cost
while keeping the worker share equal.

42
00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:59.400 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Finding the best assignment is the optimal
matching problem.

43
00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:04.760 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
With squared distance cost, an optimal
assignment exists.

44
00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:08.220 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
It divides the square into convex polygons.

45
00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:12.620 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
This simple geometry is one advantage of squared
distance cost.

46
00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:17.140 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
The same problem can be posed in higher
dimensions.

47
00:03:18.020 --> 00:03:21.580 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
In dimension 3, the unit square becomes a unit
cube.

48
00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:25.660 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Here, 3 factories sits on its front face.

49
00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:29.000 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Each colored region contains the worker

50
00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:32.640 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
assigned to one factory and extends through the
cube.

51
00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:36.080 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Each occupies one third of its volume.

52
00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:40.980 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
These regions are convex polytopes, the
3-dimensional counterparts

53
00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:44.180 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
of convex polygons. Higher dimensional

54
00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:48.840 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
geometry is difficult to visualize, especially
beyond dimension 3.

55
00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:50.800 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
So we will use 2-dimensional

56
00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:54.520 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
drawings with convex polygons to illustrate the
ideas.

57
00:03:55.940 --> 00:03:57.220 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
For the rest of the story,

58
00:03:57.660 --> 00:04:01.180 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
imagine that opposite edges of the square are
joined.

59
00:04:01.960 --> 00:04:03.420 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Leave through the left edge

60
00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:05.200 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
and you return through the right.

61
00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:08.680 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
This gives us a world without a boundary.

62
00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:15.620 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
The two-dimensional torus, called T2, with side
length one in each direction.

63
00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:20.860 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
This Distances use the shortest route, including
routes that wrap around.

64
00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:26.660 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
In dimension three, we join each face of the
unit cube to its opposite

65
00:04:26.660 --> 00:04:31.120 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
side, leaving through one face brings you back
through the opposite side.

66
00:04:31.940 --> 00:04:34.400 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
This is the 3-dimensional torus T3.

67
00:04:35.060 --> 00:04:39.680 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
In D dimensions, we make the same identification
on the unit cube

68
00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:42.340 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
to obtain Td, with side length

69
00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:43.660 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
1 in each direction.

70
00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:48.920 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
We work on the torus because it removes boundary
effects and

71
00:04:48.920 --> 00:04:52.200 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
gives us access to additional mathematical
tools.

72
00:04:52.940 --> 00:04:55.920 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
We won&#x27;t discuss these advantages in
details here.

73
00:04:56.420 --> 00:04:58.860 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
The paper explains how they enter the proofs.

74
00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:03.480 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
We now have a concrete model and a question.

75
00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:05.780 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Let&#x27;s define everything clearly.

76
00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:12.540 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Write Wn for the optimal matching cost of n
factories at X1 to Xn in TD.

77
00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:18.060 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
For each factory xi, let ai be its assigned
worker region.

78
00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:20.720 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
This region partitioned the torus.

79
00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:24.780 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
The uniform worker measure mu has total mass 1.

80
00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:29.380 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
So equal staffing means mu of Ai equals one over
n.

81
00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:35.460 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
With dx xi denoting the torus distance, the
integral adds the squared

82
00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:38.480 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
distance transportation cost across ai.

83
00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:43.900 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
We sum over all factories, then minimize over
all partitions satisfying

84
00:05:43.900 --> 00:05:45.680 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
this equal staffing requirement.

85
00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:53.820 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Because the factory locations are random, the
matching cost WN is also random.

86
00:05:54.580 --> 00:05:57.800 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Now let the number of factories n become large.

87
00:05:59.320 --> 00:06:02.280 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
How does the average transport cost depend on n?

88
00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:06.060 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
How large is the variation measured by the
variance?

89
00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:11.260 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
And after subtracting the average and rescaling
the fluctuations, do

90
00:06:11.260 --> 00:06:13.340 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
we see a fixed limiting distribution?

91
00:06:14.480 --> 00:06:17.140 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
These are the three questions we want to answer.

92
00:06:18.180 --> 00:06:22.180 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Let&#x27;s first try to understand the
expectation of the matching cost.

93
00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:26.720 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
More factories mean shorter transportation
distances, so how quickly

94
00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:29.620 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
does the expectation decrease as n grows?

95
00:06:29.620 --> 00:06:35.240 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Suppose we temporarily ignore the staffing
requirement that every factory needs

96
00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:37.180 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
to receive an equal share of workers.

97
00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:42.780 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Each worker can then go to the nearest factory.
On the d-dimensional torus,

98
00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:47.120 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
The typical distance to that factory is of order
n to the power of

99
00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:53.060 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
negative 1 over d. Since our cost is squared
distance, this gives a first guess that

100
00:06:53.060 --> 00:06:57.940 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
expectation of wn is of order n to the power of
negative 2 over d.

101
00:06:57.940 --> 00:07:02.200 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
This nearest-factory guess is incorrect in
dimension two.

102
00:07:02.820 --> 00:07:05.380 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
The blue arrow points to the nearest factory.

103
00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:10.660 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
The orange arrow points to the factory worker is
actually assigned to, which may be

104
00:07:10.660 --> 00:07:14.500 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
farther away because every factory must receive
an equal share of workers.

105
00:07:15.260 --> 00:07:20.380 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
In dimension 2, a typical worker needs to travel
a distance of order square root of

106
00:07:20.380 --> 00:07:25.120 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
log n over square root of n, instead of one over
the square root of n.

107
00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:29.240 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
For dimensions three and larger, both distance
scales

108
00:07:29.240 --> 00:07:31.840 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
are n to the power of negative 1 over d.

109
00:07:32.200 --> 00:07:35.480 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
So the nearest-factory guess has the correct
order.

110
00:07:36.360 --> 00:07:40.960 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Beyond the unexpected log n factor, there is
another surprise.

111
00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:43.140 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
In dimension 2, the leading

112
00:07:43.140 --> 00:07:45.800 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
constant is exactly 1 over 4 pi.

113
00:07:46.600 --> 00:07:51.120 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Now that we understand the expectation,
let&#x27;s look at the fluctuations.

114
00:07:52.060 --> 00:07:55.860 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Each picture has six factories, but their
locations differ.

115
00:07:56.580 --> 00:07:59.360 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
The workers are spread uniformly in all three
pictures.

116
00:07:59.880 --> 00:08:02.880 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
On the left, the factories are clustered, so

117
00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:06.260 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
workers must travel farther and the matching
cost is high.

118
00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:08.120 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
In the middle, the factories

119
00:08:08.120 --> 00:08:11.600 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
are placed randomly, giving an intermediate
cost.

120
00:08:12.340 --> 00:08:14.780 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
On the right, they are more evenly spread,

121
00:08:14.780 --> 00:08:16.400 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
giving a lower cost.

122
00:08:18.200 --> 00:08:21.500 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
The numbers show the computed optimal cost for
this

123
00:08:21.500 --> 00:08:22.440 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
particular pictures.

124
00:08:23.240 --> 00:08:27.560 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
When the factory locations are random, how large
is the variance of Wn?

125
00:08:28.540 --> 00:08:32.620 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
After centering and rescaling, do its
fluctuation approach a

126
00:08:32.620 --> 00:08:33.460 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
limiting distribution?

127
00:08:34.600 --> 00:08:37.780 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Our two papers answers these two questions and
the reveal

128
00:08:37.780 --> 00:08:41.820 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
unexpected behavior in both the variance and the
limiting distribution.

129
00:08:41.820 --> 00:08:45.940 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
In dimensions two and three, the variance has
the same scale.

130
00:08:46.600 --> 00:08:49.680 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
In dimension four, an extra log factor appears.

131
00:08:50.600 --> 00:08:52.240 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
In dimensions 5 and higher,

132
00:08:52.580 --> 00:08:55.000 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
the variance grows in a more regular way.

133
00:08:55.760 --> 00:08:58.940 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
The limiting distribution also has an
interesting transition.

134
00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:05.080 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
It is non-Gaussian in dimensions two and three,
but Gaussian from dimension 4 and onward.

135
00:09:05.860 --> 00:09:11.860 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
In these unusual low-dimensional cases, we can
also identify the exact leading constant

136
00:09:11.860 --> 00:09:12.800 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
in the variance.

137
00:09:13.540 --> 00:09:18.260 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
In dimension 2 and 3, the constants are given by
explicit lattice sum.

138
00:09:18.860 --> 00:09:22.700 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
In dimension 4, the constant has a simple closed
form.

139
00:09:23.180 --> 00:09:24.700 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Interestingly, all three

140
00:09:24.700 --> 00:09:29.960 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
constants also involve pi, just like the
expectation constant we saw earlier.

141
00:09:30.760 --> 00:09:33.980 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
These results show two different critical
dimensions.

142
00:09:34.820 --> 00:09:39.240 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Dimension 2 is special for expectation because
of the extra log n factor.

143
00:09:39.960 --> 00:09:41.960 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Dimension 4 is special for fluctuations.

144
00:09:42.860 --> 00:09:46.420 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Both the variance scale and the limiting
distribution change there.

145
00:09:47.160 --> 00:09:49.400 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
These results are both surprising and precise.

146
00:09:50.360 --> 00:09:52.040 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
How can we obtain such a detailed

147
00:09:52.040 --> 00:09:54.300 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
understanding of these unusual fluctuations?

148
00:09:55.820 --> 00:09:59.860 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Explicit limiting distributions are difficult to
obtain in random

149
00:09:59.860 --> 00:10:01.960 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
optimization problems, especially

150
00:10:01.960 --> 00:10:03.160 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
non-Gaussian limits.

151
00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:07.540 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Classical examples where such limiting
distributions are known include

152
00:10:07.540 --> 00:10:10.280 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
the exactly solvable models in the KPZ family.

153
00:10:10.920 --> 00:10:13.880 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
We won&#x27;t explain these models here, but
such

154
00:10:13.880 --> 00:10:17.400 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
precise results are possible because the exact
formula are available.

155
00:10:17.960 --> 00:10:19.680 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
For example, the formula

156
00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:25.680 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
shown here expresses the cumulative distribution
function as a Fredholm determinant. For optimal

157
00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:27.420 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
matching, the reason is different.

158
00:10:28.100 --> 00:10:31.340 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Kantorovich duality is the main tool, and
it&#x27;s the starting

159
00:10:31.340 --> 00:10:32.840 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
point of the whole argument.

160
00:10:34.280 --> 00:10:37.640 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
There are many ways to understand Kantorovich
duality.

161
00:10:38.020 --> 00:10:42.360 line:94%,end position:50%,center size:90% align:center
Here, I will use an interpretation that I find
